{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The practical-trigger variant reached the precondition yet the peer converged — consequential negative evidence. Recording it now."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cp /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft/tmp/w3/assurance_generated_test.go /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft/assurance_generated_test.go && cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Tested the practical-trigger variant that the narrowed precondition pointed at, and it did not behave as predicted: with TrailingLogs=2 the successor's log grew to 64 while its FSM lagged, its own snapshot at index 12 left the retained log starting at 13 (the precondition was met), yet the peer still converged to the leader's applied index with zero snapshot installs. This is consequential counterevidence to sufficiency of the stated precondition, so it is recorded prominently rather than deferred.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":[\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"snapshot.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":216,\\\"end_line\\\":262,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"replication.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":220,\\\"end_line\\\":300,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-installsnap17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":1814,\\\"end_line\\\":1953,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}],\n \\\"investigations\\\":[{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"Does the snapshot-boundary loop occur when a leader with non-zero TrailingLogs snapshots under a real backlog, i.e. when its retained log begins above the snapshot index, or does the peer still converge?\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"]}],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\n   \\\"id\\\":\\\"0b99111e65eb4d9faae97028d6140ff5-knowledge-0\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"compactLogsWithTrailing returns without deleting when lastLogIdx <= trailingLogs and otherwise deletes up to maxLog = min(snapshotIndex, lastLogIdx - trailingLogs), so the leader's retained log begins above the snapshot index when lastLogIdx - trailingLogs >= snapshotIndex at snapshot time. That condition is necessary for the snapshot boundary to be compacted away, but a local backlog run shows it is NOT sufficient for the rejoining peer to stay unrepairable: with TrailingLogs=2, a slow FSM and 60 uncommitted commands on the peer, the successor's own snapshot at index 12 left its store at 13..64 (boundary compacted away) and the peer still reached the leader's applied index 64 with zero Restore calls, ending with store 61..62 against the leader's 63..64.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Measured locally (non-retained) on five bootstrapped nodes in one process, InmemTransport/InmemStore, two-sided partition of {leader, partner} from the other three peers, TrailingLogs=2, SnapshotThreshold=5 with a 15 ms-per-apply FSM, 60 uncommitted commands replicated to the peer; frozen snapshot fd2d6988362c434e9fda87b87c5fb0df. The TrailingLogs=0 witnesses remain as previously recorded.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"changed_premise\\\":\\\"The previous revision treated the compaction condition as the practically decisive trigger and implied the loop would follow under a backlog. The backlog run met the condition yet the peer converged, so sufficiency is not established and the earlier implication is corrected.\\\",\n   \\\"executions\\\":{}}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\n   \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"refuted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Local construction feedback (non-retained, single run of this case) contradicts the prediction that meeting the compacted-boundary condition with non-zero TrailingLogs yields the loop. Observed: divergent_prefix with partitioned_leader_last_index 62, partner_last_index 60, partner_store 1..60, trailing_logs 2; successor_snapshot with snapshot_index 12, successor_applied_index 64, successor_last_index 64 and successor_store 13..64, i.e. the leader's retained log began above its snapshot index; then rejoin_observe with restores_before/early/late all 0, partner_applied_index 64 equal to successor_applied_index 64, partner_store 61..62 and successor_store 63..64. So the peer was repaired without any InstallSnapshot even though the boundary was compacted away, which means the TrailingLogs=0 loop cannot be generalised by the compaction condition alone: some other difference between the two setups must be load-bearing. The unexplained element is that the peer's final store (61..62) does not equal the leader's (63..64) while both report applied 64, which is consistent with the peer having snapshotted on its own after catching up (all nodes share the small SnapshotThreshold in this case), and that is recorded as the next discriminator rather than assumed. The two asserted results are unchanged in scope: both ran TrailingLogs=0 with a snapshot taken at the log end, and this receipt-less observation does not address them. Until the difference is identified, the finding must be presented as 'with TrailingLogs=0 and a snapshot taken at the log end' without any claim that a non-zero trailing configuration reproduces it.\\\",\n   \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"0b99111e65eb4d9faae97028d6140ff5-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":3,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"compaction condition necessary but not sufficient; backlog run converged\\\"}],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n     \\\"The peer reached the leader's applied index 64 with zero InstallSnapshot installs in a configuration whose retained log began above the snapshot index, so the claim that the compaction condition triggers the loop is refuted for this setup.\\\",\n     \\\"The peer's own store (61..62) differs from the leader's (63..64) at the same applied index, suggesting the peer took its own snapshot during catch-up; both this and the earlier incomplete-partition episode show how easily a harness can be read as convergence or as a loop without tracing which node acted.\\\"],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n     \\\"One local run of one case, non-retained, and the run was executed with a regex filter that skipped the other three cases in the same file.\\\",\n     \\\"All nodes share the small SnapshotThreshold in this case, so the peer could snapshot on its own during catch-up; that confound is not isolated.\\\",\n     \\\"Time in the run budget ended before the mechanism could be traced with a receipt; the four-case harness is left in the draft for a future execution.\\\"]}],\n \\\"experiment\\\":{\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\\\"harness_path\\\":\\\"assurance_generated_test.go\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"scope\\\":{\\\"description\\\":\\\"Four cases of the sealed-partition five-node history in one harness: (TrailingLogs=0, no post-snapshot entries), (10,20), (25,20) and a backlog case (TrailingLogs=2, SnapshotThreshold=5, 15 ms-per-apply FSM, 60 uncommitted commands on the peer, leader snapshots on its own while its log runs ahead of its FSM).\\\",\n     \\\"assumptions\\\":[\\\"The backlog case meets the compacted-boundary condition if the leader's own snapshot index is below its retained store's first index.\\\",\\\"Counting Restore calls identifies InstallSnapshot installs without touching production logic.\\\",\\\"Precondition guards skip rather than fail.\\\"],\n     \\\"excluded\\\":[\\\"No network transport; the peer's own snapshot behaviour under the shared small SnapshotThreshold is not disabled.\\\"],\n     \\\"parameters\\\":{\\\"peers\\\":5,\\\"cases\\\":[[0,0,5,false],[10,20,5,false],[25,20,5,false],[2,0,60,true]]}},\n   \\\"prefix\\\":\\\"For each case, MakeClusterCustom(Peers=5, Bootstrap=true, Conf{TrailingLogs, SnapshotThreshold 1<<20 or 5, SnapshotInterval 1h or 10ms, HeartbeatTimeout/ElectionTimeout/LeaderLeaseTimeout 1s}, MakeFSMFunc returning a Restore-counting MockFSM, wrapped in a 15 ms-per-apply slow FSM for the backlog case). Isolate {leader, partner} from the other three in both directions; replicate \"'`uncommitted` commands from the leader to the partner only; wait for a successor among the others; for non-backlog cases apply one command and take a user snapshot at the log end, for the backlog case fire 60 applies without waiting and wait for the leader'\"'s own snapshot index to become non-zero; apply \"'`post` further commands; heal with FullyConnect; sample each node'\"'s state, indexes and store bounds plus the partner's Restore count over ~1.5 s.\\\"}}\njson.dump(d,open('update-17.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update-17.json 2>&1 | head -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cp /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft/tmp/w3/assurance_generated_test.go /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft/assurance_generated_test.go && cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Tested the practical-trigger variant that the narrowed precondition pointed at, and it did not behave as predicted: with TrailingLogs=2 the successor's log grew to 64 while its FSM lagged, its own snapshot at index 12 left the retained log starting at 13 (the precondition was met), yet the peer still converged to the leader's applied index with zero snapshot installs. This is consequential counterevidence to sufficiency of the stated precondition, so it is recorded prominently rather than deferred.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":[\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"snapshot.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":216,\\\"end_line\\\":262,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"replication.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":220,\\\"end_line\\\":300,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"src-installsnap17\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":1814,\\\"end_line\\\":1953,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}],\n \\\"investigations\\\":[{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"Does the snapshot-boundary loop occur when a leader with non-zero TrailingLogs snapshots under a real backlog, i.e. when its retained log begins above the snapshot index, or does the peer still converge?\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"]}],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\n   \\\"id\\\":\\\"0b99111e65eb4d9faae97028d6140ff5-knowledge-0\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"compactLogsWithTrailing returns without deleting when lastLogIdx <= trailingLogs and otherwise deletes up to maxLog = min(snapshotIndex, lastLogIdx - trailingLogs), so the leader's retained log begins above the snapshot index when lastLogIdx - trailingLogs >= snapshotIndex at snapshot time. That condition is necessary for the snapshot boundary to be compacted away, but a local backlog run shows it is NOT sufficient for the rejoining peer to stay unrepairable: with TrailingLogs=2, a slow FSM and 60 uncommitted commands on the peer, the successor's own snapshot at index 12 left its store at 13..64 (boundary compacted away) and the peer still reached the leader's applied index 64 with zero Restore calls, ending with store 61..62 against the leader's 63..64.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Measured locally (non-retained) on five bootstrapped nodes in one process, InmemTransport/InmemStore, two-sided partition of {leader, partner} from the other three peers, TrailingLogs=2, SnapshotThreshold=5 with a 15 ms-per-apply FSM, 60 uncommitted commands replicated to the peer; frozen snapshot fd2d6988362c434e9fda87b87c5fb0df. The TrailingLogs=0 witnesses remain as previously recorded.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"changed_premise\\\":\\\"The previous revision treated the compaction condition as the practically decisive trigger and implied the loop would follow under a backlog. The backlog run met the condition yet the peer converged, so sufficiency is not established and the earlier implication is corrected.\\\",\n   \\\"executions\\\":{}}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\n   \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"refuted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Local construction feedback (non-retained, single run of this case) contradicts the prediction that meeting the compacted-boundary condition with non-zero TrailingLogs yields the loop. Observed: divergent_prefix with partitioned_leader_last_index 62, partner_last_index 60, partner_store 1..60, trailing_logs 2; successor_snapshot with snapshot_index 12, successor_applied_index 64, successor_last_index 64 and successor_store 13..64, i.e. the leader's retained log began above its snapshot index; then rejoin_observe with restores_before/early/late all 0, partner_applied_index 64 equal to successor_applied_index 64, partner_store 61..62 and successor_store 63..64. So the peer was repaired without any InstallSnapshot even though the boundary was compacted away, which means the TrailingLogs=0 loop cannot be generalised by the compaction condition alone: some other difference between the two setups must be load-bearing. The unexplained element is that the peer's final store (61..62) does not equal the leader's (63..64) while both report applied 64, which is consistent with the peer having snapshotted on its own after catching up (all nodes share the small SnapshotThreshold in this case), and that is recorded as the next discriminator rather than assumed. The two asserted results are unchanged in scope: both ran TrailingLogs=0 with a snapshot taken at the log end, and this receipt-less observation does not address them. Until the difference is identified, the finding must be presented as 'with TrailingLogs=0 and a snapshot taken at the log end' without any claim that a non-zero trailing configuration reproduces it.\\\",\n   \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"0b99111e65eb4d9faae97028d6140ff5-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":3,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"compaction condition necessary but not sufficient; backlog run converged\\\"}],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n     \\\"The peer reached the leader's applied index 64 with zero InstallSnapshot installs in a configuration whose retained log began above the snapshot index, so the claim that the compaction condition triggers the loop is refuted for this setup.\\\",\n     \\\"The peer's own store (61..62) differs from the leader's (63..64) at the same applied index, suggesting the peer took its own snapshot during catch-up; both this and the earlier incomplete-partition episode show how easily a harness can be read as convergence or as a loop without tracing which node acted.\\\"],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n     \\\"One local run of one case, non-retained, and the run was executed with a regex filter that skipped the other three cases in the same file.\\\",\n     \\\"All nodes share the small SnapshotThreshold in this case, so the peer could snapshot on its own during catch-up; that confound is not isolated.\\\",\n     \\\"Time in the run budget ended before the mechanism could be traced with a receipt; the four-case harness is left in the draft for a future execution.\\\"]}],\n \\\"experiment\\\":{\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"@q-backlog-trigger\\\",\\\"harness_path\\\":\\\"assurance_generated_test.go\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src-compact17\\\",\\\"src-repl17\\\",\\\"src-installsnap17\\\"],\n   \\\"scope\\\":{\\\"description\\\":\\\"Four cases of the sealed-partition five-node history in one harness: (TrailingLogs=0, no post-snapshot entries), (10,20), (25,20) and a backlog case (TrailingLogs=2, SnapshotThreshold=5, 15 ms-per-apply FSM, 60 uncommitted commands on the peer, leader snapshots on its own while its log runs ahead of its FSM).\\\",\n     \\\"assumptions\\\":[\\\"The backlog case meets the compacted-boundary condition if the leader's own snapshot index is below its retained store's first index.\\\",\\\"Counting Restore calls identifies InstallSnapshot installs without touching production logic.\\\",\\\"Precondition guards skip rather than fail.\\\"],\n     \\\"excluded\\\":[\\\"No network transport; the peer's own snapshot behaviour under the shared small SnapshotThreshold is not disabled.\\\"],\n     \\\"parameters\\\":{\\\"peers\\\":5,\\\"cases\\\":[[0,0,5,false],[10,20,5,false],[25,20,5,false],[2,0,60,true]]}},\n   \\\"prefix\\\":\\\"For each case, MakeClusterCustom(Peers=5, Bootstrap=true, Conf{TrailingLogs, SnapshotThreshold 1<<20 or 5, SnapshotInterval 1h or 10ms, HeartbeatTimeout/ElectionTimeout/LeaderLeaseTimeout 1s}, MakeFSMFunc returning a Restore-counting MockFSM, wrapped in a 15 ms-per-apply slow FSM for the backlog case). Isolate {leader, partner} from the other three in both directions; replicate \"'`uncommitted` commands from the leader to the partner only; wait for a successor among the others; for non-backlog cases apply one command and take a user snapshot at the log end, for the backlog case fire 60 applies without waiting and wait for the leader'\"'s own snapshot index to become non-zero; apply \"'`post` further commands; heal with FullyConnect; sample each node'\"'s state, indexes and store bounds plus the partner's Restore count over ~1.5 s.\\\"}}\njson.dump(d,open('update-17.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update-17.json 2>&1 | head -3\"","aggregated_output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 35, in <module>\nNameError: name 'false' is not defined. Did you mean: 'False'?\n{\"valid\": false, \"run_id\": \"8085826003f64081939807df782a8a05\", \"elapsed_seconds\": 2922.15946132, \"diagnostics\": [{\"details\": {}, \"code\": \"submission\", \"category\": \"semantic\", \"task\": \"\", \"candidate_version\": 0, \"object_ids\": [], \"paths\": [], \"material_ids\": [], \"message\": \"Submission file is missing or escapes the draft directory\", \"allowed\": []}], \"meaning\": \"Read-only validation of the